# **SEU: Making Experience Computable**
Most Customer Experience systems fail for one simple reason:

> **They observe experience, but they cannot compute it.**

Dashboards summarize.
Models predict.
LLMs explain.

But none of them operate on **experience as a first-class, stateful object**.

---

## The Core Breakthrough
> **Experience becomes computable when it has identity, state, and transitions.**

This is the same threshold that every major computational leap has crossed:
* Money became computable when value had identity and ledgered state
* Databases became computable when records had schema and lifecycle
* Distributed systems became computable when state transitions were formalized

**Customer Experience had never crossed this threshold — until now.**

---

## The Structured Experience Unit (SEU)
The **Structured Experience Unit (SEU)** is the first object that makes experience computable.

It does so by satisfying *all* the requirements of a true computational primitive.

### SEU is both:
* **The atomic unit of CX**
* **A true primitive in the computational sense**

These are not metaphors. They are properties.

---

## Why SEU Is the Atomic Unit of CX
An atomic unit must be **indivisible without loss of meaning**.

An SEU represents:
* one **canonical Experience Driver** (identity)
* aggregated evidence across mentions (state)
* measurable emotional, temporal, and operational signals

If you split an SEU further:

* you lose coherence
* you lose executability
* you lose traceability

Anything smaller is noise.
Anything larger is abstraction.

👉 **SEU is the smallest unit at which experience remains actionable.**

